BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: What Caught My Eye – Paintings by L. A. Thom
 pson
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260601T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260630T235959
UID:6a21fddec7d3cd58005e2a7d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of June\, the works of L. A. Thompson are
  on display at Forest Hill branch\nL. A. Thompson (Lou) is a Toronto-based
  artist working in acrylic and watercolour. She has been painting for over
  ten years\, transitioning from watercolour to acrylic in pursuit of great
 er vibrancy and depth.\nLou works intuitively mostly from her own photogra
 phs\, drawn to subjects that stop her and catch her eye — a landscape\, 
 a piece of bread\, a barn under a night sky. Her work has been shown at Le
 slie Grove Gallery\, with the Artists' Network\, and this is her first sol
 o exhibition at Forest Hill Public Library.\n”I take pictures of things 
 that catch my eye — and then I do my best to paint them.\nIt's as simple
  and as complicated as that. A mountain range seen through a haze. A barn 
 at the edge of a night sky. A croissant on a counter. My phone goes everyw
 here with me\, and when something stops me\, I photograph it first and thi
 nk about it later.\nMy landscapes are my main focus with so many wonderful
  ways nature displays itself.  Food found its way into my work because it'
 s genuinely beautiful — and surprisingly difficult. A baguette\, a pair 
 of pears\, three tangerines — the light\, the texture\, the weight of th
 em — it's painstaking work that demands as much attention as any landsca
 pe.\nMy work is generally more impressionistic than realistic art.  I work
  in acrylic and watercolour\, painting from photographs until each piece f
 eels right. The subjects in this show don't have an obvious connection exc
 ept that I liked what I saw - it caught my eye."\nThis exhibit is part of\
 nTPL's Art Exhibit\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for loc
 al artists to exhibit their work at locations across the city. These exhib
 its aim to reflect the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neig
 hbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed during Forest Hill branch open hour
 s.
LOCATION:Forest Hill
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a21fddec7d3cd58005e2a7d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
