BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Google Prompting Essentials: Online Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T203000
UID:6a10b7db43b41b3d006870c7@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Ready to develop the skills to use generative AI (GenAI) tools
  effectively and responsibly for work and daily life? Join our staff-facil
 itated learning circle to complete the self-paced Google Prompting Essenti
 als course. By completing this program\, you will earn a Google Prompting 
 Essentials certificate to share on your resume\, LinkedIn and social media
  profiles!\nWhat You'll Learn\nThis program is designed for those with som
 e experience with using GenAI. Through short videos\, articles\, assignmen
 ts and quizzes\, you will discover:\nEssential\nPrompting Skills\n: How to
  use a prompting framework to create a desired output with GenAI\; how to 
 evaluate and refine your results.\nEveryday Work Tasks:\nStrategies to dra
 ft content\, brainstorm ideas\, create tables\, timelines\, and more.\nDat
 a & Presentations:\nHow to explore data visualization and use GenAI respon
 sibly when working with data.\nAdvanced Techniques:\nHow to use prompt cha
 ining for complex tasks and prompt versioning to save and reuse your best 
 prompts over time.\nLearning Circles are study groups guided by\nlibrary s
 taff for people who want to complete the Google Prompting Essentials speci
 alization together in a welcoming space:\nLearners meet weekly and work to
 gether through course materials.\nParticipants are expected to commit to a
 ttending all four learning circle sessions.\nSome Experience with GenAI Re
 commended: This course is ideal for participants who have completed the AI
  Essentials course Learning Circle.\nHow to Joi\nn a Learning Circle:\nReg
 ister once on the first session of this event series\, in order to attend 
 all sessions.\nPlease sign up with a\nvalid email address\n.\nA valid TPL 
 library card is required for online circles.\nAttend all four sessions (
 online via Zoom\, date: July 9\, 16\, 23\,30 )\nRegistration is a two-ste
 p procedure. First\, participants must complete their registration through
  this page. After this step\, library staff will follow up to request addi
 tional information in order to complete the registration.\nInformation Col
 lection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the
  Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 
 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program
  registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library h
 andles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline
 @tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a10b7db43b41b3d006870c7
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Natalia Ionescu
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T235959
UID:6a35437ff56bd86e00b7bfdc@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July\, works by artist Natalia Ionescu
  are on display at Agincourt Branch.\nNatalia is passionate about finding
  beauty and inspiration in all the people\, places and situations that she
  encounters in her everyday life. She loves sharing with others what th
 e world looks like through her lens in a way that might brighten up their 
 day\, and contributing to our community in a meaningful way.\nNatalia is a
 n immigrant artist from Eastern Europe who studied design there. She arriv
 ed to Canada as an adult and built a new life here together with her fami
 ly. She spent a few years in the social work field\, and is currently wor
 king in the corporate world.\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibits 
 Space\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists to
  exhibit their work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to re
 flect the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\n
 The exhibit can be viewed during\nAgincourt\nbranch open hours.\nFree. All
  are welcome.\nImage credit: Natalia Ionescu\; watercolour and pencil\; "
 Canadian Spring"
LOCATION:Agincourt\, First floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a35437ff56bd86e00b7bfdc
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Seungjoo Seo
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260705T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T235959
UID:6a3d6c6c7e16a03d00e807f4@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July\, works by artist Seungjoo Seo ar
 e on display at Northern District Library branch in an exhibit.\nSeungjoo 
 Seo is a Toronto-based artist and 2025 graduate of Humber Polytechnic's Vi
 sual and Digital Arts program. Working across acrylic\, oil\, and mixed me
 dia\, her art explores themes of culture\, history\, individuality\, and h
 uman challenges. She exhibited at The Lost Library in Mississauga and Deca
 gram at L Space Gallery in 2025.\n"As a Korean artist\, I explore themes o
 f healing\, identity\, and personal growth. My inspiration comes from ever
 yday life and dreams—both the ones I experience in sleep and the ones I 
 strive to achieve. Through different forms of art\, I try to express parts
  of myself and show how struggles can turn into strength. My hope is to cr
 eate work that feels open and welcoming\, giving comfort and connection to
  people who may be uncertain about their future."\nThis exhibit is part of
  TPL's\nArt Exhibits Space\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space
  for local artists to exhibit their work at locations across the city. The
 se exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural interests of the city and 
 its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed during\nNorthern District\n
 Library open hours.\nImage credit: Artist Seungjoo Seo
LOCATION:Northern District\, 2nd floor art gallery
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3d6c6c7e16a03d00e807f4
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Louis Crout
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T235959
UID:6a39699a296ea565de2df509@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout July\, works by artist Louis Crout are on display a
 t Mimico Centennial branch.\nCrout holds a BFA\, Fine Art\, from the Unive
 rsity of Toronto\, and has also attended the Vancouver School of Art\, and
  Malaspina Printmakers.\n"I am inspired by the everyday beauty found where
  I live. As a printmaker\, I attempt to interpret the colour\, space and l
 ight of these scenes in the the 2-dimensional print."\nThis exhibit is par
 t of TPL's\nArt Exhibits Space\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides s
 pace for local artists to exhibit their work at locations across the city.
  These exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural interests of the city 
 and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed during\nMimico Centenni
 al\nbranch open hours.\nFree. All are welcome.
LOCATION:Mimico Centennial\, Mezzanine
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a39699a296ea565de2df509
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Alone in Density by Young Hee Sung
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260703T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T235959
UID:6a3acf1b3efcc843503590a1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Young Hee Sung is a multidisciplinary artist and the founder o
 f Laird Art Gallery in Toronto. Her practice blends Korean brush painting 
 and Western abstraction to explore impermanence\, memory\, and transformat
 ion. She has held solo exhibitions at Assembly Hall Gallery\, Laird Art Ga
 llery\, and Ajax City Hall\, and exhibited internationally in Los Angeles\
 , New Jersey\, and Seoul. She also curates collaborative projects that mer
 ge visual art with literature and community engagement.\nYoung Hee Sung is
  the founder of Brush Densityism\, a method of layering countless brushstr
 okes\, dots\, and lines to create dense fields of rhythm\, energy\, and co
 lor. Rooted in the intuitive vitality of Korean brush painting and the str
 uctured language of Western abstraction\, her work explores impermanence\,
  memory\, and transformation. Expanding into sculpture and text\, she crea
 tes participatory experiences where painting\, poetry\, and community conv
 erge in renewal.\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibits Space\nprogr
 am. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists to exhibit the
 ir work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to reflect the di
 verse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit 
 can be viewed during North York Central Library open hours.\nFree. All are
  welcome.\nImage credit: Artist: Young Hee Sung\; Uphill.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd floor Gallery space
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3acf1b3efcc843503590a1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
