 folk\, blues\, pop\, light acoustic rock\, seafaring songs\, jazzy show tu
 nes\, country\, and bluegrass music\, by Andy Fedely on the harmonica and 
 guitar\, and Karen Dares on the violin!\nDrop-in.
LOCATION:Parkdale
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a40326f0e851028002da276
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Swati Namjoshi
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260731T235959
UID:6a24321846d5f64f7dce27e6@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July\, works by artist Swati Namjoshi
  are on display at Bloor Gladstone Library in an exhibit.\nSwati is an Ind
 ian multidisciplinary artist\, now based in Toronto\, making it her new ho
 me. She strongly believes in the power of stories and works towards illus
 trating authentic & mindful representations of cultures\, characters\, pla
 ces\, and perspectives. In her curious exploration of a gendered society\
 , Swati creates autobiographical digital illustrations in black & white\; 
 a vulnerable expression of her experiences as an artist\, a woman\, and a\
 nmother.\nSwati states "The parallels I see across cultures and the inter
 sectionality in our identity influence my art and the way I explore and em
 brace diversity.  The human experience is far more shared than it is div
 ise\, and I paint to contribute to the love rather than the hate. My style
  exhibits emotive characters embedded in their whisical worlds.  I believ
 e presenting vulnerable experiences through hopeful illustrations that toy
  with reality and exaggeration\, could nurture a sense of belonging and co
 mmunity."\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit Space\nprogram. Toro
 nto Public Library provides space for local artists to exhibit their work 
 at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cu
 ltural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be v
 iewed during Bloor/Gladstone branch's open hours. Please check the\nBloor
 /Gladstone branch\npage before you visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\nImage c
 redit: Swati Namjoshi\; Bounce\; Medium: Digital Illustration Printed on P
 aper
LOCATION:Bloor/Gladstone\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a24321846d5f64f7dce27e6
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Photography by Alex Curren
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260731T235959
UID:6a1ee10d4407827f8aa208a6@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July\, the works of artist Alex Curren
  are on display at\nDeer Park branch\n.\n"Alex Curren is a Toronto-born ar
 tist working across film\, photography\, and design. After studying animat
 ion in Ottawa\, he returned to the city where he grew up and continued his
  studies in film at York. He has served as cinematographer on several shor
 t films and a feature by an emerging filmmaker. He has previously sold sil
 kscreen prints\, drawings\, and handmade goods. He now works at a midtown 
 photo lab\, continuing to explore everyday beauty through analog and digit
 al processes."\n"This project began when I moved back to Toronto in 2017. 
 I told a friend the city felt unphotogenic. He replied\, “It’s easy to
  make art in a far-off place— every blade of grass feels exotic.” Coul
 d I find beauty in the familiar? These film photographs\, all taken near t
 he Deer Park Library\, reflect my love of walking\, photography\, and the 
 quiet intersections of nature and the urban. This collection is an invitat
 ion to pause and see Toronto anew\, a city known for its hectic pace.\nThi
 s exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit Space\nprogram. Toronto Public Lib
 rary provides space for local artists to exhibit their work at locations a
 cross the city. These exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural interes
 ts of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed during D
 eer Park's open hours. Please check the\nDeer Park branch\npage before you
 r visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\nImage credit: Artist: Alex Curren\nTitl
 e of Piece: Picasso Rock (Oriole Park)\nMedium: Photography (film)\nSize
 /Dimensions: Large
LOCATION:Deer Park
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a1ee10d4407827f8aa208a6
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Elaine Logashov
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260701T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260731T235959
UID:6a381bd7f56bd86e00b825c5@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July\, the works of Elaine Logashov a
 re on display at Oakwood Village Library and Arts Centre.\nElaine loves ex
 ploring the gritty\, urban parts of the city - alleyways\, neglected corne
 rs\, and places where signs of human life linger in grime. What\nstarted a
 s a hobby has grown into a passion\, with more time dedicated to creating 
 art. Inspired by urban decay and overlooked spaces\, the work\ncaptures be
 auty and emotion in the worn-down and forgotten.\nI see beauty in the over
 looked and worn parts of the city - alleys\, grime\, and signs of human li
 fe. My work captures the grit and decay of urban spaces\,\nespecially in T
 oronto\, which has both gritty\, hidden corners and polished\, upscale are
 as. I leave my pieces purposely unnished to reect how these\ngritty places
  are often second thought and unnoticed. Art is my way of seeing the beaut
 y in the neglected and the overlooked\, and sharing that\nperspective with
  others.\nThis exhibit is part of\nTPL's Art Exhibit\nprogram. Toronto Pub
 lic Library provides space for local artists to exhibit their work at loca
 tions across the city. These exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural 
 interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed d
 uring Oakwood Village Library and Arts Centre open hours.
LOCATION:Oakwood Village Library and Arts Centre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a381bd7f56bd86e00b825c5
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
